From 96bdc28efc6554da78a34163f8a46944d297123b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 07:49:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] fix(cors): override Vercel's static-storage wildcard;
 correct a false attribution
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

THE MISATTRIBUTION

Every HTML page on this site is served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`.
ops/DOMINATION-RUNBOOK.md recorded that, twice, as a Vercel dashboard header
rule the owner had to go and remove. That was an inference from one true fact —
the header appears in no file in this repository — to a conclusion that was
never tested, and it sent them looking for a switch that does not exist.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY HAPPENING

Vercel attaches the wildcard to everything it serves out of static/prerendered
storage. Platform behaviour, not configuration. The discriminating measurement
is WHICH routes carry it: `/`, `/refer`, `/commercial` and `/robots.txt` all do,
and every one of them is `○` static or `●` prerendered in the build output;
function-rendered routes that do not set CORS themselves carry nothing.

Two consequences, both non-obvious:

  · Middleware could never have removed it. The CDN adds the header after
    middleware has run, so a delete there is a no-op.
  · A local check cannot prove the fix. `next start` never sets the header at
    all, so any local test passes vacuously. Production or nothing.

THE FIX

`headers()` cannot unset a header, but a declared header becomes part of the
build output routing config and overrides the platform default. Publishing the
canonical origin instead of `*` has the same practical effect: a page on another
origin can no longer read the response body.

Scoped by negative lookahead so the machine editions keep their wildcard —
/llms.txt, /llms-full.txt, /ai.txt, the .md mirrors and /api/knowledge are
deliberately cross-origin readable, an agent fetching them from a browser
context needs it, and verify-vercel-config fails the build if they lose it.
/api/ is excluded because those routes are function-rendered and the public ones
set their own CORS. vercel.json keeps owning those paths, so no route emits two
values.

The exclusion pattern was tested against Next's own path-to-regexp before
shipping: it matches /, /refer, /commercial, /robots.txt, /sitemap.xml and every
prerendered page, and does not match /llms.txt, /ai.txt, /api/knowledge or
anything ending in .md. SITE_URL derives from NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL rather than
being a second copy of the site's own address.

Runbook §2 rewritten to say what was wrong and why, rather than quietly deleting
it — including what to do if the override does not win, and the fact that the
odd permissions-policy alongside it was only a stale pre-P0 deployment.

44 guards pass. tsc unchanged from main. Verified in production or not at all:

  curl -sI https://ecowoods.ca/         | grep -i access-control   → https://ecowoods.ca
  curl -sI https://ecowoods.ca/llms.txt | grep -i access-control   → *

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JnM1SffeC3zX5W7tcVWUJ3
