From d89836285abf865197e8baae497bedf51c8553c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 10:03:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] fix(links): every link now has to prove it lands
 somewhere
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three defects, one new guard, and one instrument the repo did not have.

WHAT WAS WRONG

1. /admin/projects/new returned a 404, and it was a primary button.
   The "+ New Project" action on /admin/projects pointed at a route that does
   not exist. It fell through to /admin/projects/[id], looked up a project with
   the id "new", found none and called notFound(). Projects are not created
   that way at all — they are created by converting an accepted quote
   (admin/quotes/[id]/ConvertToProjectForm). The button now goes there.

2. FeedbackBand offered an estimate link that did nothing on two pages.
   The band asks "did you find what you were looking for?", and on "no" it
   offers the estimate form via href="#estimate". Six of the eight pages that
   render it also render <EstimateForm>. /refer and /resources do not. On those
   two, a visitor who had just said "no, I did not find it" clicked "Request
   one" and the page did not move.

   estimateHref is now a required prop with no default. A component cannot know
   which page it is on, so it stops guessing: each caller states where its
   estimate lives, and the guard resolves that string against the page that
   passed it. A default would put the guess back AND hide it from the check.

3. Two profile URLs were hardcoded in the footer, outside the verified list.
   SiteFooter's own header comment states the policy — "never invent a handle",
   "an icon renders ONLY when a real profile URL is set" — directly above two
   entries that carried instagram.com/ecowoodshardwood and
   facebook.com/ecowoodshardwood as string literals. PROFILE_LINKS, the file
   that records which profiles have been opened and confirmed and the array that
   feeds sameAs, holds the same two URLs. So the same fact lived in two files
   and could drift: correct a handle in the constants and the schema changes
   while the footer does not, and the site tells Google one thing and a visitor
   another about the same profile. The footer now reads PROFILE_LINKS.

   Neither URL has a read date, and both carry the retired brand's handle.
   That is not something code can settle — runbook §9.

scripts/verify-destinations.mjs — THE OUTBOUND QUESTION

verify-links.mjs asks whether every route has a way IN; it found /papers and
/authority in the sitemap with no path to them. Nothing asked whether the links
we publish resolve. Six checks, one pass, 486 links:

  · every internal href reaches a page.tsx, a route.ts or a file in public/
  · every dynamic slug is IN ITS MANIFEST, not merely shaped like one.
    /guides/white-oak-flooring passes any regex and 404s in production; only
    lib/guides.ts knows the difference
  · every #fragment is an id rendered on the destination — resolved per page,
    including the layouts, so a fragment in shared chrome is checked against
    every page that renders it
  · no internal link points at one of our own alias redirects
  · nothing links to ecowoodshardwood.com
  · every external host comes from PROFILE_LINKS or the short infrastructure
    list, so a profile cannot be linked without also being claimed in sameAs

Four things it had to learn, each of which had made it silently blind:

  comments      SiteFooter explains in prose that its city links "used to be
                href='#areas'". Read as a live link, that is a bug report about
                a fix from a year ago.
  object form   { label: 'Stairs', href: '/hardwood-stairs-toronto' } is not an
                edge case: it is how the whole navigation is written. Header.tsx
                declares 62 destinations that way. Before this the guard
                verified 383 links while having read neither the header nor the
                footer — the links on every page.
  dynamic()     home-client.tsx loads FloorCatalog and MachineCatalog via
                dynamic(() => import(...)). A scanner reading only static
                imports concludes no page renders them, then passes their
                anchors because it has nothing to check them against.
  layouts       Header and SiteFooter are mounted in app/layout.tsx, not in any
                page. A map built from page.tsx alone contains neither.

Each of those is a false PASS, which is the failure mode that matters: nobody
investigates a green check. Six negative tests are recorded in the PR body.

The redirect list is obtained by calling next.config.js's redirects(), not by
reading it. The commercial aliases are generated from an object, so a regex over
`source:` sees six rewrites, none of the redirects, and reports "no self-
inflicted hops" with total confidence about the one thing it was asked.

scripts/audit-rendered.mjs — WHAT A BROWSER CAN SEE AND SOURCE CANNOT

playwright and @axe-core/playwright have been devDependencies for some time and
nothing used them. A page whose source is impeccable can still, at 390px, scroll
sideways, put a 28px tap target under a thumb, scroll an anchor to a heading
that then sits behind the sticky header, or carry a horizontal rail no keyboard
can move (WCAG 2.1.1). Those are measurements, and a browser is the only
instrument that takes them.

  pnpm audit:rendered                    → 20 routes × 4 viewports → audit/rendered.md

Validated both directions against fixtures before shipping: a page built with
one of each defect produced exactly one finding per defect; a clean page
produced none. Same control probe as crawl-site.mjs — an environment that
cannot reach the site, or has no browser binary, says so and reports nothing.
Deliberately NOT in `pnpm verify`: a build guard that needs the network is a
build that fails for reasons unrelated to the commit.

VERIFY: 45 guards green (44 + destinations). tsc 107 errors, byte-identical to
the main baseline, all downstream of the Prisma client not generating in a
sandbox. tsc caught one real mistake of mine during this work — a call site I
reverted while negative-testing — which is the argument for running it.

