From 5e50d5044268b82c47e82a785d05d6b47384ef6b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 14:41:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] =?UTF-8?q?fix(mobile):=20the=20top=20strip=20was=20sc?=
 =?UTF-8?q?rolling=20every=20page=20sideways=20=E2=80=94=20and=20the=20aud?=
 =?UTF-8?q?it=20that=20found=20it=20was=20crying=20wolf?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

TWO THINGS HERE. The instrument was wrong about most of what it reported, and
it was right about something bad.

WHAT THE FIRST RUN SAID, AND WHY MOST OF IT WAS NOISE

Against production: 62 horizontal-overflow, 565 tap-target, 619 findings on 20
pages. That is not a report. Nobody reads it, and the three real defects inside
it are invisible. Two detector faults, both mine:

  1. OVERFLOW BLAMED CLIPPED ELEMENTS. It walked every element and accused
     anything sticking out past the viewport. Two of the three culprits per page
     were not culprits:

       .mobile-sheet  the nav drawer — position:fixed, translateX(100%),
                      visibility:hidden. Parked off-canvas until opened. It is
                      SUPPOSED to be at x=320.
       .svt-track     a marquee inside .svt-viewport, which is overflow:hidden.
                      Wider than the screen by design, clipped by its own parent.

     An element can only widen the document if nothing between it and the root
     clips it. Any ancestor with overflow-x other than `visible` — hidden, clip,
     auto, scroll — ends the propagation. The detector now measures the real
     overflow first (scrollWidth − clientWidth), then attributes it only to
     elements that survive that walk, groups siblings under the row that
     actually causes it, and says "unattributed" when nothing survives instead
     of naming the nearest wide thing to have something to name.

  2. TAP TARGETS USED THE AAA NUMBER AS A FAILURE LINE. 44×44 is WCAG 2.5.5 and
     Apple's HIG. This site is held to AA, which is 2.5.8: 24×24. At 44 a 42px
     button fails and so does every link in a footer list — hence 565. Below 24
     is now a finding; 24–43 is counted per page and never itemised.

Validated both ways before shipping, against fixtures carrying the exact decoy
shapes: a page with an off-canvas drawer, a clipped marquee and a keyboard-
reachable rail produces ZERO findings; the same page plus one nowrap row and one
20px target produces exactly those two.

WHAT IT WAS RIGHT ABOUT

  .ub-phone sits at 329→430px inside a 320px viewport. The utility strip does
  not fit, so the document scrolls sideways ON EVERY PAGE OF THE SITE.

That is not cosmetic. Every section is then ~110px wider than the screen, text
runs off the right edge mid-sentence, and the visitor drags the page back and
forth to read one line. It is the worst thing a layout can do on a phone and it
was on the home page.

Three fixes, each measured in a browser against the real .ub rules, not reasoned:

  · 720px → 900px. The full six-item strip — area, separator, hours, three
    links, phone, every one white-space:nowrap — needs about 896px. At 768 it
    overran by 128px and a tablet scrolled sideways too, hidden by a breakpoint
    chosen by eye.
  · below 430px the two nav links go as well, leaving the hours and the phone
    number. The block already stated that principle — "keeps the two facts that
    convert and drops the rest" — and then dropped three of six, which is enough
    at 720 and not at 320. Service areas and Reviews are in the footer and the
    mega-menu on every page, exactly as its own note says.
  · the hours truncate with an ellipsis (min-width:0, or text-overflow never
    fires). If HOURS_LINE_SHORT is ever lengthened the hours shorten and the
    phone number stays put, rather than the document growing a scrollbar again.

And the targets in that strip: the links were 15px tall and the phone number
20px, against an AA floor of 24. The phone number is the highest-value target on
the site. .ub-inner was already min-height:38px, so the room was there and
nothing was claiming it — vertical padding rather than a height, so the text
stays centred at any font size.

After: the strip is clean at 320, 390, 768 and 1280 — no overflow, nothing under
the AA floor, five advisory targets between 24 and 44, which is correct for a
compact utility strip.

ALSO: --disable-dev-shm-usage. /dev/shm is 64 MB in a container and Chromium
puts renderer shared memory there; overrunning it is the "Target crashed" that
killed the first run in the Codespace. Plus one newPage retry, because a crashed
tab is a memory blip, not a fact about the site.

STILL OPEN, and the next clean run will name them: 2 keyboard-unreachable scroll
rails, 2 unfocusable widgets, 12 unreachable route/viewport pairs (likely the
admin routes 307-ing to login, which is correct behaviour the script should
learn to recognise).

VERIFY: 45 guards green.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JnM1SffeC3zX5W7tcVWUJ3
